  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1052 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThe much-advertised late season heat wave begins today, as very\nstrong H5 ridging becomes centered over the area. The latest\nensemble guidance continues to show H5 heights around 600 dm and\n850mb temperatures peaking around 20-22C each afternoon through\nat least Thursday. Both values are at the climatological \nmaximum for the time of year. This will yield maximum \ntemperatures in the middle to upper 90s (possibly isolated low\n100s) and minimum temperatures in the lower to middle 70s \nduring this time. Both the forecast high and low temperatures \nwill approach or break record hot/warm values this week. See the\nClimate section below for more details. Humidity values will be\ngreatest today and Wednesday, with dew point temperatures \nreaching the lower 70s. This will yield widespread heat index \nvalues around 105 degrees today and Wednesday. The current Heat \nAdvisory runs through 7 PM CDT Wednesday.\n\nOn Thursday and Friday, dew point temperatures will begin to \ndraw downward into the middle to upper 60s due to moisture being\nextracted from the soil. Air temperatures will still be in the \nupper 90s for most locations, but there is more uncertainty \nregarding if the heat index values will breach the triple \ndigits. However, given the late season nature of the heat and \nimpacts to outdoor activities, an extension of the Heat\nAdvisory through the end of the work week is possible. \n\nThe H5 ridge will begin to break down this weekend, but high\ntemperatures will still reach the lower to middle 90s on\nSaturday. However, there is better model agreement that some\nnoticeable relief from the heat will arrive beginning Sunday\ninto early next week as frontal boundary attempts to move south\nthrough the area. This will yield high temperatures in the upper\n80s to lower 90s on Sunday and lower to middle 80s on Monday.\nAdditionally, there may be enough moisture and forcing from the\ncold front to kick off isolated to widely scattered showers and\nthunderstorms on both days.",
